 { In life the best play is often to make a short-term sacrifice in exchange for greater rewards down the line. The same holds true on the chessboard, and in this section and the next we'll be taking a look at some of the most common of these situations. The current section will cover the typical benefits you can play for by sacrificing a pawn or a piece, including things like opening up lines, gaining outposts, activating your pieces, etc. In the final section of the chapter, we'll focus on the important 'exchange sacrifice' when one side gives up their rook for a minor piece. Let's begin! } 30... { -KEY- } e4 $1 { In the 2016 World Championship, Magnus Carlsen uncorked this powerful move with the Black pieces against the challenger, GM Sergei Karjakin. } ( 30... Qb8 $6 { It was perhaps tempting to continue in 'safer' fashion, but after } 31. Bd2 Qb6 32. Bxe3 fxe3 33. g3 $15 { Black may stand objectively better, but the presence of opposite colored bishops makes the task of winning the game near-impossible. } ) 
31. dxe4 { White has no choice but to accept the pawn sacrifice. } ( 31. Bxf6 $2 { Already we can see the practical problems of dealing with Black's last move. The exchange of bishops loses on the spot. } gxf6 $1 32. dxe4 Qe5 $1 { White is lost as there are too many lines of attack against his king. } ) 
Bxc3 32. Rxc3 Qe5 $1 { This is the position that Carlsen had in mind. In exchange for just a pawn, Black has turned his queen into a monster. At the same time, the knight on e3 can no longer be challenged since the dark-squared bishops have been forced off the board. } 33. Rc1 Ra8 34. h3 h6 35. Kh2 Qd4 36. Qe1 { White misses a good opportunity to equalise, instead choosing a more passive continuation that would soon land him in serious trouble. } ( 36. e5 $1 { White should have copied Black's idea. Now after } Qxe5 37. Bd3 $1 { material would be equal but White would have gotten a much-needed boost in the activity of his bishop. } ) 
Qb2 37. Bf1 $2 { In an incredibly difficult position and low on time, Karjakin goes fatally wrong. } ( 37. Be2 $1 { was the only saving path, not an easy move to find. The point is that after } Ra2 { White has an incredible sequence to hold the balance: } 38. e5 $3 { when after } Qxe2 39. Qxe2 Rxe2 { the unexpected blow } 40. b6 $3 { holds the draw, since after } cxb6 41. exd6 Rxg2 42. Kh1 Rd2 43. d7 Rxd5 44. Rc8 Kh7 45. d8=Q $11 Rxd8 46. Rxd8 $11 { the endgame is drawn. } ) 
Ra2 { The pressure on g2 is overwhelming. } 38. Rxc7 { In desperation, White grabs a pawn. } Ra1 { White resigned, since if the queen moves then after 39...Nxf1+ mate will follow soon. In this game, Magnus was able to stir up practical chances by sacrificing just a pawn. The advantages he got were open lines, and the trade of dark-squared bishops. This second point made it impossible for White to challenge his powerful outposted knight on e3. } 
