 { In the first example we saw Carlsen giving up a pawn in exchange for open lines and an outpost. In the following position, the bishop pair will also be a factor in the decision to sacrifice. } 1... { -KEY- } Qc7 $1 { Instead of giving his opponent the ideal structure and a free hand on the kingside, Black sacrifices a central pawn. } ( 1... Nxg3 $2 { would be a big mistake. } 2. hxg3 Qc7 3. Re2 $16 { Killing Black's only good plan. White can continue to build up a kingside attack in the coming moves, while Black has no way of generating counterplay. It's a little hard to believe, but objectively White is already close to winning! } ) 
2. Nxe4 dxe4 3. Bxe4 Nc2 $1 { Now Black forces the gain of the bishop pair. } ( 3... Bxe4 $5 { If the move 3...Nc2 wasn't an option, this path would also secure something close to equality. In this case the point would be that after } 4. Rxe4 Qc2 $1 5. Qxc2 Rxc2 $11 { Black may not have won the bishop pair, but has managed to activate all of his pieces. } ) 
4. Qxc2 Qxc2 5. Bxc2 Rxc2 $11 { Black was equal in the online game Carlsen,M - Theodorou,N (2023). This type of sacrifice is as risk-free as they come, since there is a mix of pluses gained like the bishop pair, weak pawns to target, greater piece activity, and securing of the seventh rank. } 
