 { Besides opening up of lines, another situation where it pays to scan for a pawn sacrifice is when you are struggling to finish developing your pieces. In this theoretical position, Black has to worry about their lack of space in the centre and queenside pressure along the h1-a8 diagonal. Can you find a good way of sacrificing a pawn to open lines & accelerate your development? } 8... { -KEY- } Nc6 $1 { Correct! The entire theory has been built up around this pawn sacrifice, with the Black players working out that they will get enough activity to make up for the missing material. Let's take a look at how play may unfold. } ( 8... a6 $6 { To understand the power of the sacrifice, it's worth taking a look at this move, as it has been the most popular choice of the Black side in the thousands of games to have reached this position. } 9. b3 { Stabilising the pawn chain. } Qc7 10. Bg5 { White is close to setting up the ideal piece configuration, with the heavy pieces coming to the central files. } Nc6 $2 { Black finally steps out the knight to c6, supported by the queen. However, the loss of time is disastrous. } 11. Bxf6 $1 Bxf6 12. Nd5 Qd7 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. Nxf6 $1 ( 13... Nc6 13. Nb6 $6 { is not so convincing, since after } Qb7 15. Nxa8 Bxa1 $11 { Black has succeeded in simplifying the position while keeping material equal. } ) 
exf6 15. Qd4 $16 { Black's structure is in ruins, while White will continue to ramp up the pressure by bringing their rook to d1 next. } ) 
9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bxc6 { Grabbing the pawn is the true test of this idea, since otherwise Black would complete their development with ease. } Rb8 { For the cost of a pawn, Black is now the side with the queenside pressure! } ( 10... Bh3 $6 { is less convincing, since after } 11. Bxa8 Qxa8 12. f3 Bxf1 13. Kxf1 $16 { White is a lot closer to consolidating their position and running away with the extra pawn. } ) 
11. Bg2 Qa5 $1 { Another precise move! Black understands that White wants to play b2-b3, and so they play a move to prevent this. } ( 11... Be6 $2 { would lose all compensation in a flash, since now White could reply with } 12. b3 $1 { when their structure is bulletproof. Unfortunately for Black, there are no tactics along the vulnerable a1-h8 diagonal. For example: } Ne4 13. Bxe4 Bxc3 14. Bh6 $1 Re8 15. Rc1 Qa5 16. Rc2 $16 { White's structure is as solid as it gets, and so Black is just down a clean pawn. } ) 
12. Qc2 { Looking for b2-b3 once again. } Bf5 $1 { Provoking the e-pawn into advancing. } 13. e4 Be6 14. b3 { White finally saves the pawn, but now the tactics do work for Black! } Nxe4 $1 15. Bxe4 Qxc3 $11 { Black recovers the pawn and equalises the position. This type of sacrifice may not be especially glamorous, but it's worth being familiar with such themes as sometimes it is the only means to escape the opening with healthy piece development. So far we have covered situations where a positional pawn sacrifice may be called for. In general, gambiting a pawn is something that most chess players are comfortable with since it's a small amount of material. Where it gets trickier is when the position demands that you sacrifice a piece! Let's explore several examples of this in play now. } 
