 { Much like in the case of a pawn sacrifice, the motivation for a piece sacrifice is to gain one or several different positional pluses. The main difference is that the rewards need to be higher to justify giving up a larger amount of material. Oftentimes, a piece sacrifice will be made easier by the fact that you will collect several pawns along the way. Let's take a look at the current position for an example of this. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxc5 $1 { White has to sacrifice, otherwise Black will obtain their ideal setup and condemn White's rooks into permanent passivity. } ( 1. Bc1 $2 { White needs to be careful to avoid passive play, as they will reach a losing position. } e6 $1 2. Red2 $2 { Now the attempt to break down Black's pawn structure fails. } Nxc4 $19 { Black is objectively winning, since the knights and structure combine to overpower White's exchange advantage. } ) 
bxc5 $2 ( 1... Kd7 $1 { This move was the trickier defense, and would have forced White to continue the sacrificial approach. } 2. Bxd6 $1 { Offering the bishop for a second time! This one is harder to pull the trigger on. The point is } exd6 3. c5 $1 { A deep pawn sacrifice. By forcing ...bxc5, the knight on a5 is left undefended. This proves important in some variations where White enters with a rook on e7 and follows up with Rxa7. } bxc5 4. Rde1 Kc7 5. Re7 Kb6 6. Rxf7 $18 { White's rooks coordinate extremely well, while Black's king is vulnerable. Note also how the knight on h5 has been left paralysed. Finding the most precise moves in this sequence would be very tricky, and on the harder end of the type of lines you will have to calculate in your games. } ) 
2. Rxe7+ { White will likely pick up the d6 and f7 pawns next, when the invading rooks are much more powerful than the opponent's scattered knights. } Kb8 3. Rxd6 { One of the luxuries that White had in the starting position was being up an exchange, enabling them to sacrifice without ending up down in material. In fact, on a strict material count White is up due to the number of pawns collected. } Nxf4 4. Rxf7 Nxc4 5. Rdd7 { Doubled rooks on the seventh are usually fatal, and here is no exception. } Ne2+ { Black goes about collecting a few pawns, but now their unstable knights will get picked off. } 6. Kf2 Nxc3 7. Rb7+ Kc8 8. Rfc7+ Kd8 9. Rxc5 Rh4 10. Rbc7 $18 { There is no way to save both knights. In this example we saw how White was prompted in several ways to make the sacrifice. Activating the rooks, targeting the king, and collapsing the Black pawn structure were all only possible by giving up the bishop. } 
