 { In the first example we saw piece activity, structural damage, and targeting the king as the mix of factors leading to White giving up the bishop. In the online game Carlsen,M - Naroditsky,D (2023) the same motivations were at play. Find the strongest move for White. } 1. { -KEY- } Nd5 $1 { The central knight sacrifice is a popular and often devastating weapon in Sicilian positions. } exd5 ( { Declining the piece sacrifice with } 1... Nxd5 $2 { is miserable for Black, since after } 2. exd5 $18 { White has enormous pressure along the e-file, without being down any material. } ) 
 ( 1... Qd8 $2 2. Nxf6 Qxf6 3. e5 $18 { is also winning for White. } ) 
2. exd5+ { Correct! White is down a full piece for just a pawn. Such a sacrifice can be scary to play. Let's investigate why not only does White have full compensation, but they are in fact objectively winning! } Kd8 ( 2... Be7 $2 3. Nf5 $18 { wins back the piece on the spot. } ) 
3. Bg5 $1 { White continues developing as if nothing happened. Why is White able to play calmly without an obvious way of regaining the piece? The reason lies in the cumulative effect of all of his positional pluses. He has: control of the open e-file, constant pressure against the enemy king, the possibility to cripple the kingside pawns with Bxf6+, an advanced outpost on c6, the option to open more lines with the c2-c3 pawn break, the weak pawn on b4 as a target. White also has full piece development, while two of Black's minor pieces have not yet moved. The pawn on d5 also works to limit the activity of Black's bishop. In a nutshell, Black has so many fires to put out that White does not need to rush to recover their missing material. } Nd7 4. Nc6+ { With this move Magnus will win the bishop pair and gain a passed pawn on c6. } ( 4. c3 { was another winning direction, opening lines on the queenside. } ) 
Bxc6 { The knight is too strong to be left standing. } 5. dxc6 Nc5 6. Bxf6+ { In the game, Magnus played Qd1-d4 which is also strong, but forcing damage to the structure is even stronger. } gxf6 7. Qd4 Be7 8. Qxb4 $18 { White has two pawns for the piece, one of which is a powerful passed pawn on c6. All of his pieces are active, with his rooks ready to control both the d- and e-files. Black is barely up material, while remaining stuck with a long list of positional problems. Pulling the trigger on such sacrifices is tough, as you can't calculate all the possibilities until the end. Recognising that each positional advantage gained adds up and that their combined effect can ultimately overpower the opponent is key to making the right judgment. } 
