 { Space & central control are also major drivers of positional sacrifices. In the following position, taken from the game Polgar,J - Spassky,B (1993) , Black understood that he cannot continue to accept the central space squeeze: } 20... { -KEY- } Nbxd5 $1 { The sacrifice will not only activate all of Black's pieces, it will also give them a mobile pawn centre with dangerous d- and e-pawns. } 21. exd5 Nxd5 ( { Black has two pawns for his piece, reducing the risk involved. Besides this, he has the immediate threat of } 20... Nxd5 20. -- Nxe3 { , since } 22. Qxe3 { is not possible due to } Rxc2 { . } ) 
22. b4 Nxb4 $6 { It's always tempting to secure a third pawn when you've sacrificed a piece, since now material is roughly equal. } ( 22... Nxe3 $1 { was best, when Black is at least equal because of tactics along the a1-h8 diagonal. } 23. fxe3 Bxf3 24. gxf3 e4 $1 25. f4 { White should give up the exchange to stabilise their position. } Bxa1 26. Rxa1 Qf6 $13 { The position is really complicated, but if any side has the harder practical problems to solve it's White since they are the side with a weaker pawn structure and a more vulnerable king. } ) 
23. Bb3 $14 { In spite of even material, White does stand better for now. This is because Black is for now unable to advance his central pawns. } Bd5 $1 { Correctly assessing that the pawns cannot be pushed for now. The threat is 24...Bxb3 next, followed by 25...Nc2. } ( 23... d5 $6 24. Qd2 $1 e4 25. Nd4 $1 { Forming a dark-squared blockade of the pawns. } Nd3 26. Red1 $16 { Somewhat surprisingly, White is able to maneuver around the knight while Black does not have any great answer to the threat of a2-a4 next. I suspect Spassky analysed variations like this one when deciding against pushing the central pawns. } ) 
24. Red1 $6 ( 24. Rec1 $1 { was necessary, to control the c4 square and stop Black's strong idea. } ) 
Bc4 $1 { Now it becomes much easier to advance the pawns. } 25. Qd2 ( 25. Bxc4 $2 { cannot be played, as then White would get steamrolled by the pawns after } bxc4 $17 ) 
Nd3 26. Bh6 d5 $1 { The position is roughly equal according to computers, but for a human this is much more pleasant to play as Black. Over the coming moves White fails to hold the balance and the pawns continue their advance: } 27. Bc2 Nc5 28. Re1 Bh8 29. Rad1 Qb6 30. Qg5 $6 { White worsens her position with an overly aggressive queen move. } e4 31. Qe3 { Accepting that it's necessary to retreat to deal with the action in the centre. } Bxa2 ( { But not } 31... exf3 $4 { because of } 32. Qxe8 Rxe8 33. Rxe8 ) 
32. Bb1 Bxb1 33. Rxb1 Na4 34. Qxb6 Nxb6 { Black had reached a winning position as the mass of pawns outgun the piece. An important thing to note is that Black really did not have much of a choice but to sacrifice in this game, since quieter moves would have left White with a crushing space advantage and much better piece activity. This situation is quite common, and it pays to be quick to sense the danger. Let's look at another example of this type of 'no-choice' sacrifice next. } 
