 ( { The most natural options here are probably } 0... Nxb6 0. fxg5 { or } 1. Nxg4 { . However, both seem to leave Black in command. Given this, can you find a better (and more aggressive) alternative for White? } ) 
1. { -KEY- } Nxf7 $1 { Even if you are unsure that the piece sacrifice works, it will often be worth it to risk the uncertainty of complications versus accepting a quieter but much worse position. } ( 1. fxg5 $2 { leads to immediate disaster. For example: } N4xe5 2. dxe5 Qxg5 $19 { Black is winning. } ) 
 ( 1. Nxg4 $6 { is less bad than 1.fxg5, but Black will end up a pawn to the good after } Bxg4 2. fxg5 Qxg5 $17 ) 
Kxf7 2. fxg5+ Kg8 3. Bxg6 hxg6 4. Qxg6 { Correct! This is the position that White took aim for at the beginning. If you are a more advanced player, perhaps you have seen that from here Black cannot escape the threat of perpetual (checks on f7 and h5 with the White queen) and at the same time rescue the stranded knight on g4. } Rxe3 { Black has nothing better than to accept the draw. } ( 4... Qe7 $6 { This move blocks the perpetual for now, but after } 5. h3 { now 5...Nxe3 fails since after 6.Rf3! the knight is stuck, so Black has to play } Rf8 6. Rxf8 Qxf8 7. hxg4 $11 { In the end Black ends up down a pawn, although the position should be a draw with best play given White's inferior structure. } ) 
5. Qf7+ Kh8 6. Qh5+ Kg8 7. Qf7+ $11 { In the comment after 1.Nxf7! I said that “even if you are unsure that the piece sacrifice works, it will often be worth it to risk the uncertainty of complications versus accepting a quieter but much worse position”. But how exactly can you judge whether a risk is worthwhile or not? It turns out that we can rely on a mathematical principle that is used in other games, such as poker or in stock trading. It’s known as Expected Value (EV) and it tells you the average outcome of a decision if repeated many times. Returning to the choice between 1.Nxg4 and 1.Nxf7. We can fully calculate the first path, and we know we’ll end up down a pawn. The move 1.Nxf7 however leads us into murkier waters, and we know there’s a chance we might end up with a perpetual check but we also understand that we may have overlooked something. A good way to approach this dilemma is to estimate your chances in the case of the simpler line. Since after 1.Nxg4 we’ll be down a pawn, we might guess that against that particular opponent we have only a 10% chance of winning, 20% chance of drawing, and 70% chance of losing. So over 100 games we would only on average score 20 points, for an EV = 0.2 This context helps when deciding whether to go for 1.Nxf7 or not. Instead of arriving at a decision only through solving the line in full, we can also approach the problem by estimating roughly how confident we are in the lines we have tried to calculate. Let’s say, for example, that we think there’s a 50% chance that there is a perpetual check after all, while the rest of the time we’ll lose. In such a case, we’ll draw half the time and lose the rest. Over 100 games, that would score 25 points total or - put differently - the move 1.Nxf7 has an EV = 0.25 This shouldn’t be too surprising a result, since the first path of playing down a pawn with no compensation was pretty miserable. And as the saying goes: “desperate times call for desperate measures!”. Anyway, we’ll leave this detour here. I just wanted to mention it in case you would like to explore this way of approaching problems more in the future. I can assure you that, from conversations I’ve had with Grandmasters over the years, they regularly lean on this type of probability-based thinking to help them make good decisions in the face of uncertainty. Let's now close out the section with our final example, which will give us an opportunity to explore the idea of expected value further. } 
