 { Black is facing two threats: 2.Bg4 winning the queen, and winning the pawn on e5. Find the only sequence for Black that keeps the position rougly balanced. } 1... { -KEY- } exd4 $1 ( 1... exf4 $6 { has the same idea, but it turns out that opening the f-file favors White. For example: } 2. Bg4 fxe3 3. Qg2 Kc7 4. Bxf5 Nxf5 5. Rf1 { White is much better organised here, keeping good winning chances. } ) 
 ( 1... Kc7 $2 { Sidestepping the pin does not offer good chances. } 2. dxe5 $16 { White is up a key central pawn, and has easy improving play. Black is losing. } ) 
2. Bg4 { White has nothing better than to grab the queen. } dxe3 { Correct! Black goes down a queen in exchange for a collection of positional pluses. } 3. Bxf5+ Kc7 $1 ( 3... Nxf5 $2 4. Qxd5 { should be avoided. } ) 
4. Qxe3 Nxf5 $13 { We reach a complex middlegame, with equal chances for both sides. Black has only two pieces for the queen, but they have a large list of positional pluses. These include the better structure, safer king, pressure on the open e-file, and better piece coordination. Black may have relied on the concept of 'expected value' discussed recently. Black may have estimated their chances of scoring points after 1...Kc7 2.dxe5 as very low, and therefore played the queen sacrifice since even if they were not sure that it was a good idea, all they needed to determine is that it was the lesser evil. This brings us to a close for the section. We'll end the chapter with a very popular technique: the exchange sacrifice. } 
